now the archbishop of canterbury is  today calling for thousands of hectares  of land  owned by the church of england to be  used to build affordable housing he's  also calling for the government to do  more to provide affordable homes too  well i'm delighted to say the archbishop  justin welby joins us now so just tell  us what is the idea behind this report  from the church of england  the report's an independent report  chaired by mr charles alvarez  and the uh who's had lifetime experience  in housing with a very powerful group of  very diverse people  and the vice chair is the bishop of  kensington who uh  in whose areas the gram is grenfell  tower it's an independent report  which has worked for two and a half  years and looks at  the crisis in housing the eight billion  people in inadequate housing the poor  housing  the uh diversity uh  issues that mean that people in uh  black minority ethnic communities have  had worse outcomes in the pandemic and  so on  and it provides great challenges to the  church of england  to the government to individuals and to  landowners it's a  very powerful report setting a vision  for 20 years of housing it's a very  wide-ranging report as well  um a list of things you want the  government to do from increasing housing  benefit to committing to more affordable  homes  how big an impact have benefit cuts had  and would you like to see the 20 pound a  week increase to universal credit  maintained  i think the the report mainly focuses  on the issues around affordable housing  and  the 20-pound benefit is something that  a number of bishops have spoken up in  favor of and certainly when you look at  the demand on food banks and  areas like that we need to try and  strengthen people who are really up  against it  but the issue today is affordable  housing not just available housing  we've tried to set out a vision in the  way that  was set out in the 1940s for health  we know in the health service that the  aim is that health should be delivered  free  at the point of need good health care  free at the point of need  everyone agrees on that it's cross party  it is the objective  there is no equivalent in housing and  uh there is no definition of affordable  housing the minister said that in the  house of lords last week  there is uh a need for  every part of society we talk to the  government or the  report it's not us yet and though i very  much welcome and support the report  it talks to the government but it also  challenges the church and the challenge  to the church is where is your soul  where are your values  do you believe what jesus said that you  love  god with all your heart soul strength  and mind and you love your neighbor as  yourself  because if you do you are going to want  stable  sociable sustainable safe  and secure housing uh right  across society and that's the challenge  to the church to use its land and be a  leader in this area yeah of course so  many of the  issues that people experience with  housing have just been exacerbated  in the last year with lockdowns a  pandemic meaning you're spending more  time  at home of course and you're talking  about the challenge to the church but  this is also clearly  a challenge to government as well do you  feel that you're having to step in  because the government hasn't done  enough  it's not this government uh this is the  housing crisis  goes back at least to the mid-1970s  um and you know kathy come home  uh you'd you'd nerve that film  um ken loach right back in the 60s  talking about poor housing that had an  immense effect at the time  but we have far more people in private  rented accommodation  uh then than we do now  and we have far more people a far higher  proportion of the population in  inadequate housing now  than we did then and so  this isn't a about this government or  its  immediate predecessors it goes right  back through  all parties and we need a national  common vision for housing and that will  be sacrificial  the report calls on the the um  church to be sacrificial not to take its  maximum possible benefit  that is the challenge that i welcome and  support  and i affirm that call and call on the  church to react in that way  and it calls on government and  landowners in the same way  okay um you mentioned grenfell in your  first answer  do you think more needs to be done to  remove dangerous cladding from high  rises  uh absolutely i do and uh  that is one of the keynote  recommendations in the report  uh that the government  the report recommends as i say it's  independent but i support this that the  uh the the government take  responsibility for the cladding  uh they've announced 3.2 billion i think  a couple of weeks ago but that  may well not be enough and then recover  that from developers and other people  okay now i just want to ask you a little  bit about coronavirus because of course  we're all waiting for the prime minister  to announce  the road map out of lockdown i know  there's lots of concern about  the amount of school that children have  been missing  what would you like to see tomorrow  well a rate map is a very important  thing  but it has to be one that we can be  really confident about  so if you know as well as i do that this  last  lockdown has been one of the most  painful for the most  people um i go into a local hospital as  an  assistant chaplain and you see  the exhaustion of the staff the way  they're worn down  we need a road map that will stop us  going up and down and i know the  government is deeply committed to that  the huge  success of the vaccine program uh  speaks to what we can do and  i think it needs to be secure  and that we know it is going to be  delivered so we can all move forward i  mean  the pandemic has stripped the paper off  the cracks which we've covered up  there's a schools crisis because  children have missed a year of education  had a disrupted year not all missed it  thanks to the heroism and the quality of  of the schools um there's a housing  crisis which is  revealed being revealed in its full  extent uh  the health service is desperately tired  there is a huge need for  a clear road map and i'm sure the  government is  working on that and will produce that  are they right to prioritize the  reopening of schools  i'm absolutely sure they're right to  prioritize the opening of the schools  that is probably the most urgent thing  it's been the most urgent thing right  the way through i remember talking to  one of the senior medical advisors last  year he said because  the church of england educates more than  a million children  uh in its schools and he said whatever  you do  do everything you can to keep the  schools going because the damage to  children's  mental health their long-term education  is so  is so serious you mentioned the success  of the vaccine rollout in the uk and i  know that you've previously been quite  critical of countries buying up more  vaccines than they need  at what point do you think the uk should  donate  vaccines to poorer countries so many of  which haven't vaccinated anyone at all  well i'm the the anglican communion of  which  technical senior bishop in the world is  80 million people in 165 countries  and the average anglican is a woman in  their 30s on less than four dollars a  day  probably in an area of conflict or  persecution  so most of my work is is with  the very poor around the world i'm very  pleased that not only has this  government done  very very well on the vaccine program  but it's also leading the world  on the international distribution of  vaccines  and i welcome that warmly i hope that  others  follow that example bill gates has  called for that  um and is himself putting huge sums of  money into this  i hope that uh across europe we do this  as soon as they've sorted out their own  problems  on basic distribution of vaccine and i  hope places like canada and the united  states do that  we keep saying this won't be defeated  anywhere until it's defeated everywhere  and certainly in many parts of the world  they need  massive support with this and we are  doing  very well um it's the first time i've  interviewed you so i have a couple of  other  questions if i may um you said last  summer in the wake of the black lives  matter protest that the church would  very carefully review statues at some of  its major places of worship  canterbury cathedral or westminster  abbey and then you said that some will  have to come down  some names will have to change that was  eight months ago  have you removed any statues with links  to the slave trade and do you think  that's something the church should  continue to look at  i'm really pleased that there have been  very few that needed to be removed that  couldn't  simply be put in a better in putting  context and the context the statue  explained  canterbury thankfully hasn't had to take  anything down i really welcome that  i think in total something like two have  been put in museums or something  uh across the whole country and we're  talking tens of thousands  um so that that's  very very good news but we come  back to uh the underlying issue here  which is  slavery and the legacy of slavery the  impact it had on so many people and so  many parts of the world and still has  on the minority i think populations  through racism in this country  and we have to put resources in  to supporting them and demonstrating  that we are  all equal in this country that there is  an end to injustice race racial  injustice among others  in this country that's part of the  reason for the housing report  i'm talking about modernizing the church  would you like to see a woman  archbishop one day  the church and people in the church be  very relieved to know that i have no  influence on my successor  i'd be delighted i'd be delighted what i  want to see is  um diversity of leadership in which  leadership is anointed by the holy  spirit of god and  in which they love jesus christ and they  love their neighbor and enable  millions of people to discover the love  of christ for them  and then just finally of course the  royal family in the church have a very  strong and historic connection were you  sad to see the news that the duke and  duchess of sussex are going to step back  from royal duties  uh i i i don't comment on  uh on the royal family in public have  anything to say it would be said in  private  okay that is fair enough um archbishop  thank you so much for coming on the  program and talking about a very  important  report into housing such a key issue of  course  for so many people at the minute so  thank you thank you  oh just talking about housing uh the  ministry of housing have sent us this  statement  in response to the archbishop's report  we welcome and encourage the practical  steps that the church is taking to make  more of their land available for  affordable housing  we've delivered over 500 000 new  affordable homes since 2010  we're supporting renters by extending  notice periods and banning bailiff  enforcement of evictions  and we're spending billions of pounds to  remove cladding  so people's homes are safe

